</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Fingerprint Generation via Zeroth-order Gradient Estimation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once the query set is constructed, the next step is to generate the LLM’s fingerprint. This process involves three substeps: <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> querying the target LLM, <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> embedding the inputs and outputs into a continuous vector space, and <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3)</span> applying a regression method to estimate the Jacobian matrix, which serves as the final fingerprint.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Querying the Target LLM.</span> The procedure begins by feeding the base queries <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and their corresponding perturbed counterparts <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x^{\prime}_{i,j}\}_{j=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x^{\prime}_{i,j}\}_{j=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> to the target LLM, <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. To mitigate the inherent randomness of LLM outputs, each query is sent to the model <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> times, yielding a set of textual responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Embedding Inputs and Outputs.</span> We then employ a pre-trained sentence embedding model to transform all inputs and outputs into high-dimensional vectors. For each query, the final output embedding is computed by averaging the embeddings of the <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> responses, resulting in a stable representation. Let <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> be the sentence embedding function. The embedded vector for the base input <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{x_{i}}=E(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{x_{i}}=E(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and its averaged output vector is <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{e}_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{e}_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, the vectors for a perturbed query are <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{x^{\prime}_{i,j}}=E(x^{\prime}_{i,j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{x^{\prime}_{i,j}}=E(x^{\prime}_{i,j})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{e}_{y^{\prime}_{i,j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{e}_{y^{\prime}_{i,j}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Estimate the Jacobian Matrix.</span> With these vectors, we can formulate the Jacobian estimation as a linear regression problem. For each base query <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the input and output difference vectors for all <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> of its perturbations:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Input difference:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>Input difference:</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Input difference:}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9X.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta x_{i,j}=e_{x^{\prime}_{i,j}}-e_{x_{i}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta x_{i,j}=e_{x^{\prime}_{i,j}}-e_{x_{i}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E9Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Output difference:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>Output difference:</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Output difference:}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9Xa.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta y_{i,j}=\bar{e}_{y^{\prime}_{i,j}}-\bar{e}_{y_{i}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta y_{i,j}=\bar{e}_{y^{\prime}_{i,j}}-\bar{e}_{y_{i}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We seek to find a local Jacobian matrix <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> that best models the linear relationship <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta y_{i,j}\approx J_{i}\Delta x_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta y_{i,j}\approx J_{i}\Delta x_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given the practical constraints on the number of queries allowed, the number of perturbation samples <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is often small relative to the dimensionality of the Jacobian matrix. This can lead to an underdetermined system, making an ordinary least squares estimate unstable. To address this, we use Ridge Regression, which introduces an L2 regularization term to produce a more robust estimate of the Jacobian:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}=\arg\min_{J}\sum_{j=1}^{m}\|\Delta y_{i,j}-J\Delta x_{i,j}\|_{2}^{2}+\alpha\|J\|_{F}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>J</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}=\arg\min_{J}\sum_{j=1}^{m}\|\Delta y_{i,j}-J\Delta x_{i,j}\|_{2}^{2}+\alpha\|J\|_{F}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the regularization hyperparameter and <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Frobenius norm. Finally, a Jacobian matrix <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed for each of the <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> base queries. The overall fingerprint for the model <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is the element-wise average of these individual Jacobian matrices. This aggregation step creates a more comprehensive and stable fingerprint that captures the model’s characteristic behavior across a diverse set of inputs.</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\mathcal{M}}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}J_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\mathcal{M}}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}J_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">With the final fingerprints generated for the source and suspicious models, the comparison is performed by calculating their similarity using Eq. (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 3rd item ‣ 4.2. Overview of ZeroPrint ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Reading Between the Lines: Towards Reliable Black-box LLM Fingerprinting via Zeroth-order Gradient Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) to determine copyright attribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Evaluation</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmark Models.</span> The primary benchmark models in our experiments are from <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LeaFBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LeaFBench</span> is built upon 7 mainstream pre-trained foundation models, including variants of Qwen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2024c</a>)</cite>, Llama <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, Mistral <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, Gemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2024a</a>)</cite>, and TinyLlama <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. These models were selected with parameter counts ranging from 1.1B to 14B. <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LeaFBench</span> also implements various LLM post-development techniques, including fine-tuning, model merging, distillation, different system prompts, RAG, adversarial attacks, etc. In total, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LeaFBench</span> provides 149 different model instances and establishes a representative benchmark for evaluating fingerprint-based LLM copyright auditing. The details of <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LeaFBench</span> can be found in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Implementation Details ‣ Reading Between the Lines: Towards Reliable Black-box LLM Fingerprinting via Zeroth-order Gradient Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> For the baseline methods, we implement 3 untargeted black-box methods, LLMmap <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pasquini et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Model Equality Testing (MET) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and Sentence Embedding Fingerprinting (SEF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, and 1 targeted black-box method, TRAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gubri et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We also include a white-box method, REEF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, for reference. Note that targeted methods, including TRAP, are typically <em id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semi-black-box</em>. They assume black-box access to the suspicious model, but white-box access to the source model, which is a slightly stronger assumption than untargeted methods. The maximum number of queries is set to <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=200</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Settings of </span><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZeroPrint</span><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span> For default settings, we select <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math> base queries from HumanEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and generate <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=4</annotation></semantics></math> perturbed queries, with a total number of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> queries. We input each query to the LLM <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=20</annotation></semantics></math> times, leading to <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> queries in total. For the sentence embedding model, we choose all-mpnet-base-v2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reimers and Gurevych, 2019</a>)</cite>, which is a classic yet powerful model also used in LLMmap. More detailed settings are in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Implementation Details ‣ Reading Between the Lines: Towards Reliable Black-box LLM Fingerprinting via Zeroth-order Gradient Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span> In our experiments, following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, we primarily leverage two metrics: <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> Area Under the ROC Curve (<span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AUC</span>) and <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> Partial AUC with FPR<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in[0,0.05]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in[0,0.05]</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">pAUC</span>). The AUC provides a holistic measure of a method’s ability to distinguish between related model pairs (a source model and its derivative) and unrelated pairs across all possible classification thresholds <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, the pAUC metric is specifically designed to assess performance by focusing on a specific low false-positive-rate (FPR) region (<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i.e." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i.e.</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,0.05]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,0.05]</annotation></semantics></math>) of the ROC curve. Higher AUC and pAUC scores indicate superior performance of a fingerprinting method.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06605v1/querynumbers.svg" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/132;" width="471" height="132"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>The performance of <span id="S5.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZeroPrint</span> with different numbers of queries. We study three factors: the number of base queries <math id="S5.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of perturbed queries <math id="S5.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, and the number of repeats per query <math id="S5.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.3. Query Set Construction ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Reading Between the Lines: Towards Reliable Black-box LLM Fingerprinting via Zeroth-order Gradient Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the overall performance comparison between <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZeroPrint</span> and various baseline methods. In addition to the primary metrics of AUC and pAUC, we also report the True Positive Rate at a 1% False Positive Rate (TPR@1%FPR) and the Mahalanobis Distance (MD). The TPR@1%FPR metric measures the true positive rate that a method can achieve while maintaining a low FPR of 1%. The MD metric measures the separability of the fingerprint distributions, reflecting the distance between positive pairs (a source model and its derivatives) and negative pairs (independent models). A larger MD indicates a stronger ability to distinguish between related and unrelated models. For both TPR@1%FPR and MD, higher values signify better performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results demonstrate that <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZeroPrint</span> achieves SOTA or comparable performance across all evaluated metrics. It significantly outperforms existing untargeted black-box methods like LLMmap, MET, and SEF. Notably, <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZeroPrint</span> also shows superior performance compared to the targeted, semi-black-box method TRAP, improving the AUC to 0.720 and the pAUC to 0.683. However, a performance gap remains when compared to white-box methods like REEF, which have direct access to model internals. It is worth noting that these results were achieved under a strict query limit of 200 queries per model. Subsequent experiments in our ablation study reveal that <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZeroPrint</span>’s performance can be further enhanced by increasing the number of queries allowed. This scalability is further detailed in Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Ablation Study ‣ 5. Evaluation ‣ Reading Between the Lines: Towards Reliable Black-box LLM Fingerprinting via Zeroth-order Gradient Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we study the effects of several important hyperparameters or elements in <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZeroPrint</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effects of Different Numbers of Queries.</span> The total number of queries is a critical factor influencing the performance of <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZeroPrint</span>. This number is determined by three key hyperparameters: the number of base queries (<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>), the number of perturbed queries generated for each base query (<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>), and the number of repeated generations for each query (<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>). To understand their impact, we conducted a series of experiments, varying one parameter at a time while keeping others fixed. The results are illustrated in Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.1. Experimental Settings ‣ 5. Evaluation ‣ Reading Between the Lines: Towards Reliable Black-box LLM Fingerprinting via Zeroth-order Gradient Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effects of Different Numbers of Base Queries <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> We first investigated the impact of <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> by setting its value to 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5, with <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math> being the default in our main experiments. As shown in the first subplot of Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.1. Experimental Settings ‣ 5. Evaluation ‣ Reading Between the Lines: Towards Reliable Black-box LLM Fingerprinting via Zeroth-order Gradient Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the performance of <span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZeroPrint</span> remains relatively stable across different values of <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. The AUC and pAUC fluctuate in a small interval. This observation suggests that the number of base queries may not have a significant impact on the effectiveness of <span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZeroPrint</span>. A small number of base queries (<math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e.g." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e.g.</annotation></semantics></math>, 2) is sufficient to generate a distinctive fingerprint.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effects of Different Numbers of Perturbed Queries <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> Next, we examined the influence of <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> with values set to 1, 2, 4, 9, and 14. The default value used in our main experiments was <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=4</annotation></semantics></math>. The results, depicted in the second subplot of Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.1. Experimental Settings ‣ 5. Evaluation ‣ Reading Between the Lines: Towards Reliable Black-box LLM Fingerprinting via Zeroth-order Gradient Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, reveal that <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> has a pronounced effect on the method’s performance. When <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is small (<math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e.g." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e.g.</annotation></semantics></math>, 1 or 2), the estimated gradients are less stable, leading to inconsistent results (<math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e.g." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e.g.</annotation></semantics></math>, a high AUC but a low pAUC). However, as m increases to 4 and beyond, both AUC and pAUC show a clear and significant upward trend. The performance peaks at <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=14</annotation></semantics></math>, achieving the highest AUC (0.778) and pAUC (0.706) in this set of experiments. This trend highlights the potential of <span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZeroPrint</span> to achieve even greater effectiveness and robustness with an increased query budget, as more perturbed queries provide a more accurate gradient estimation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effects of Different Numbers of Repeats per Query <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> Finally, we analyzed the effect of <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, testing values of 1, 10, 20, 30, and 40, with <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=20</annotation></semantics></math> as the default setting. This parameter is designed to mitigate the inherent randomness in LLM-generated outputs. The third subplot of Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.1. Experimental Settings ‣ 5. Evaluation ‣ Reading Between the Lines: Towards Reliable Black-box LLM Fingerprinting via Zeroth-order Gradient Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that when <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is small, performance suffers due to this stochasticity, yielding an AUC of only 0.607 for <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the performance improves substantially, with the AUC reaching 0.720 at <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=20</annotation></semantics></math>. Beyond this point, the results begin to plateau and fluctuate slightly. This indicates that at <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=20</annotation></semantics></math>, the averaged output embedding for a given input becomes sufficiently stable. Further increases in <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> offer diminishing returns.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>The performance of <span id="S5.T2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZeroPrint</span> using base queries from different datasets.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:292.4pt;height:31.6pt;vertical-align:-13.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-25.8pt,2.8pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
<table id="S5.T2.4.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T2.4.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;">TruthfulQA</td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;">SQuAD</td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;">DROP</td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;">Wikipedia</td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HumanEval (Ours)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.4.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AUC</span> <math id="S5.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;">0.622</td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;">0.665</td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;">0.676</td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;">0.698</td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.720</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.4.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">pAUC</span> <math id="S5.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;">0.668</td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;">0.666</td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;">0.651</td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;">0.647</td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.75pt 3.4pt;"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.3.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.683</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effects of Different Query Data.</span> The choice of data for constructing base queries is another crucial element. We evaluated the performance of <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZeroPrint</span> using base queries derived from five different datasets: HumanEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>)</cite> (our default), three question-answering (QA) datasets (TruthfulQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, SQuAD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajpurkar et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, and DROP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dua et al., 2019</a>)</cite>), and one text completion dataset (Wikipedia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Foundation, [n. d.]</a>)</cite>). The results, presented in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.3. Ablation Study ‣ 5. Evaluation ‣ Reading Between the Lines: Towards Reliable Black-box LLM Fingerprinting via Zeroth-order Gradient Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, show that the source of the query data significantly impacts fingerprinting effectiveness. When using queries from the QA datasets, the performance was consistently poor. Similarly, the Wikipedia dataset, despite being a completion task, also failed to achieve comparable results to HumanEval. This can be attributed to the nature of the tasks. Pure text completion is inherently more diverse and less structured than code completion. Thus, various derivative models tend to exhibit less consistent behavior on text-based prompts, making it harder to extract a stable and reliable fingerprint. In contrast, the structured nature of code completion elicits more uniform responses, leading to better performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effects of Different Sentence Embedding Models.</span> We investigated the influence of the sentence embedding model on <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZeroPrint</span>’s performance. We experimented with five different models of varying scales and embedding dimensions (details in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Implementation Details ‣ Reading Between the Lines: Towards Reliable Black-box LLM Fingerprinting via Zeroth-order Gradient Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). The results, detailed in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3. Ablation Study ‣ 5. Evaluation ‣ Reading Between the Lines: Towards Reliable Black-box LLM Fingerprinting via Zeroth-order Gradient Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, reveal a clear trade-off related to the embedding dimension. When the dimension is too small, the model fails to adequately capture the semantic nuances of the sentences, leading to poor performance. Conversely, a larger embedding dimension increases the size of the Jacobian matrix to be estimated. Given the constraints on the number of queries, estimating this larger matrix becomes less accurate, which also degrades the overall fingerprinting effectiveness. This is supported by the similar performance of MPNet and EmbeddingGemma, which share the same embedding dimension and achieve comparable results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3. </span>The performance of <span id="S5.T3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZeroPrint</span> using different sentence embedding models. “Dim” signifies the dimension of the generated sentence embeddings.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T3.4" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:288.6pt;height:41.4pt;vertical-align:-18.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-27.5pt,3.9pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
<table id="S5.T3.4.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T3.4.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 2.6pt;"><span id="S5.T3.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.4.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 2.6pt;">Model2Vec</td>
<td id="S5.T3.4.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 2.6pt;">MiniLM</td>
<td id="S5.T3.4.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 2.6pt;">MPNet <span id="S5.T3.4.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(main)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.4.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 2.6pt;">EmbeddingGemma</td>
<td id="S5.T3.4.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 2.6pt;">Qwen3</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.4.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.75pt 2.6pt;"><span id="S5.T3.4.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dim</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.4.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.75pt 2.6pt;">256</td>
<td id="S5.T3.4.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.75pt 2.6pt;">384</td>
<td id="S5.T3.4.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.75pt 2.6pt;">768</td>
<td id="S5.T3.4.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.75pt 2.6pt;">768</td>
<td id="S5.T3.4.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.75pt 2.6pt;">1024</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.4.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 2.6pt;"><span id="S5.T3.4.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AUC</span> <math id="S5.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
